Z||000000||Seanh||05-15-2001||08:40 PM||||I have been noticing this for a while, since I got the engine in the car. When I first got the engine installed (3.4L Camaro w/fiero upper-end) I don't remember this happening, but I COULD be wrong. When I start it in the morning, or anytime the engine has had time to cool down, It takes a while to start. I notice some white smoke upon starting also. It never lasts more then a few seconds, maybe 3-5 seconds at most usally less. Is that completely normal? But it seems to take about 5-6 seconds of cranking time before it starts up. If I start it up right after driving it, while its still warm it usally starts up after about 1-2 seconds of cranking time. Is that long crank time normal, or perhaps a fuel system problem. I have a new distributor, module, pickup coil, ignition coil, plugs, cap and rotor, o2 sensor, timeing is correct. Anyway, any ideas on what could be wrong? Ask me anything that may be helpful to figureing out this problem. BTW, I am going to unplug the battery and clear codes to see if they reset any of them cuz I have like 5 or 6 codes on there, mostly cuz of me messing with sensors. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||05-15-2001||09:50 PM||||Sean, Starting out with fresh RAM in the computer is wise.  If you have any codes address those 1st as they can cause a cascaded symptom effect and at that point you're guessing.  You'll have to use all of your senses here... What does the white smoke smell like?  I know you will look like a dog chasing another in heat, but try to determine what the cause of the white smoke is, gas, oil or coolant.  Have someone start the car cold and you sniff the exhaust pipe. (not too close and no I'm not kidding). Keep us posted (I know you will).  GL<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||1
